  "overview": {
    "text": "The weak nuclear force, also called the weak interaction, is one of nature’s fundamental forces. Unlike the strong force, it does not bind protons and neutrons; instead, it can change a quark’s type, converting a neutron into a proton or vice versa. This makes it central to beta decay, in which a nucleus can change its number of protons and become a different element. The force is carried by the charged W boson and the neutral Z boson; their large masses give the interaction an extremely short range. It is also significant because electroweak theory connects the weak and electromagnetic forces as aspects of one interaction.",
    "source_urls": [
      "https://www.energy.gov/science/doe-explainsthe-weak-force",
      "https://home.cern/science/physics/z-boson/"
    ]
  },
  "key_facts": [
    {
      "text": "In beta-minus decay, a neutron changes into a proton and the process produces an electron and an electron antineutrino.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://www.energy.gov/science/doe-explainsthe-weak-force"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "The W boson carries electric charge; the Z boson is neutral.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://www.energy.gov/science/doe-explainsthe-weak-force",
        "https://home.cern/science/physics/z-boson/"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "The large masses of the W and Z bosons limit the weak force to an extremely short range—about 10⁻¹⁸ metres, according to CERN.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://home.cern/science/physics/z-boson/"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "In the 1960s, Sheldon Glashow, Abdus Salam, and Steven Weinberg developed the theory connecting the weak and electromagnetic forces.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://home.cern/science/physics/z-boson/"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "CERN reports that W and Z bosons were first observed in 1983, supporting a key part of the Standard Model.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://home.cern/science/physics/z-boson/",
        "https://home.cern/science/physics/standard-model/"
      ]
    }
  ],
